{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>But.</b> I have already mentioned in the book that nefesh and neshamah and ruach are one name for the supernal human soul, which endures forever and does not die; and it is called nefesh and also ruach, because it is not visible to the eye. And because the poet was full of the spirit of wisdom and the Name was engraved upon his soul, therefore he said: \"But Elohim will redeem my soul from the hand of Sheol\" — it is not possible that he means he will not die like the rest of mankind, for who is the man who will live and not see death. And this is the meaning of \"will take me\": that his soul will cleave to the supernal soul, which is the soul of the heavens; and like it: \"for Elohim took him\" (Genesis 5:24), and afterward, \"in honor You will take me.\""], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The final phrase quotes Psalm 73:24 (וְאַחַר כָּבוֹד תִּקָּחֵנִי); 'the book' likely refers to Ibn Ezra's larger philosophical work, possibly Yesod Mora."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:31:23.071091984Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}